{
"dur": 49.099,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 3.64,
"text": "The Romans also started their year in March, around the spring equinox.",
"end": 3.915
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 3.915,
"vo_start": 4.19,
"vo_end": 10.91,
"text": "Rome's first calendar had just ten months and three hundred and four days. The middle of winter simply didn't belong to any month.",
"end": 11.185
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 11.185,
"vo_start": 11.46,
"vo_end": 18.578,
"text": "Back when Rome was still a kingdom, its second king added two new months: Januarius and Februarius.",
"end": 18.854
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 18.854,
"vo_start": 19.128,
"vo_end": 24.341,
"text": "But the calendar still didn't match the sun. Over time, festivals drifted into the wrong seasons.",
"end": 24.616
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 24.616,
"vo_start": 24.891,
"vo_end": 31.531,
"text": "Hundreds of years later, Julius Caesar fixed it with the Julian calendar, and made January the first the first day of the year.",
"end": 31.806
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 31.806,
"vo_start": 32.081,
"vo_end": 41.319,
"text": "From then on, Romans welcomed the year with offerings to Janus, the two-faced god of doorways, who looks back at the old year and forward to the new. January is named after him.",
"end": 41.594
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 41.594,
"vo_start": 41.869,
"vo_end": 48.149,
"text": "And here's the strange part: they always made sure to do at least a little work on New Year's Day. Yes, really.",
"end": 49.099
}
],
